And of course, pudding farming doesn't actually take so much time that
it would really go into realm of patience. My last character who
farmed puddings was a wizard who lacked spellbooks of identify and
magic mapping and was short in wishes as well as in magic markers.

It took only about 2500 turns or something like that to produce the
needed spellbooks as well as a couple extra benefits (~30 HP more,
cone of cold, a point of protection). 

I'm under impression that many players waste much, much more time
moving items between stashes. That, by the way, is a behavior that I
don't have enough patience to do. The only characters of mine who even
have stashes are wizards who leave spellbooks on some nice eyable
level (quest bottom or Fort are my favorites). And also there's an
occasional pre-bag of holding potion shop that weights too much to
carry along.

BTW, the wizard that I mentioned before took 40758 moves to ascend, it
being the third longest ascension of mine that I have data about. (The
others were another wizard with 54162 moves and a tourist with
41028). I didn't save the data on my all-time longest ascension, but
it was somewhere around 70000 turns. An average ascension takes me
about 33000 turns. 


